"""Vendored nameplate engraving geometry -- native sketch line-loops.

Provenance: the maker's-plate engraving ("Wm. Gaertner & Co -- Chicago, U. S. A."
with the scroll cartouche) hand-traced by pathedits.com from the same book ch.26
p.71 macro photo the earlier engraving was traced from, delivered as a vector DXF
and vendored at ``cad/references/nameplate-engraving.dxf``. This module is that
DXF re-imported as plain coordinate loops, so ``build_nameplate`` can draw it with
SolidWorks **sketch primitives** (closed line chains) with no DXF import at build
time.

Re-import recipe (offline, ezdxf + shapely): explode the nested block INSERTs to
WCS, flatten every SPLINE/ELLIPSE/LWPOLYLINE outline (the HATCH fills are
redundant), drop the plate outline + pinstripe frame (the two full-width
rounded rects, drawn parametrically here) and the four corner screw rings (cut as
functional holes), keep the lettering + cartouche contours, uniform-scale them to
the engraving footprint (80 mm wide, centred on the plate centre 50,27.5) and
resolve the even-odd ink region into clean exterior/hole rings.

Coordinates are plate millimetres in the build frame (origin = plate corner,
+X width, +Y height); each loop is implicitly closed (no repeated end vertex).
This is the source of truth for the engraving -- hand-edit deliberately; the
even-odd nesting (outer glyph/ornament loops vs counters) must be preserved.
"""

